    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d72">

                <pb facs="0066" n="56" sameAs="frus:frus1949v09/pg56"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/8–1749</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Douglas</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-08-17">August 17,
                        1949</date>.<lb/>[Received August 22.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#flush">No. 1357</p>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to transmit herewith,
                    with reference to the Embassy’s telegram No. 3240 of August 16, 7 p. m.,<anchor
                        n="11" xml:id="d72-f1"/> copies of the following documents:</p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>Letter from Ambassador Douglas to Foreign Secretary Bevin dated July 22,
                            1949.<anchor n="12" xml:id="d72-f2"/>
                    </p>
                    <p>Letter from Foreign Secretary Bevin to Ambassador Douglas dated July 22,
                            1949.<anchor n="13" xml:id="d72-f3"/>
                    </p>
                    <p>Memorandum from the Foreign Office dated August 15, 1949, entitled
                        “China.”</p>
                </quote>
                <p>The memorandum from the Foreign Office was handed to Mr. Ringwalt<anchor n="14"
                        xml:id="d72-f4"/> and me by Mr. M. E. Dening, Assistant Under-Secretary for
                    Foreign Affairs in charge of Far Eastern matters, who stated that the memorandum
                    represented interdepartmental views on the China problem but not necessarily
                    ministerial opinion, as some of the Ministers concerned, including Mr. Bevin,
                    the Foreign Secretary, and Sir Stafford Cripps, the Chancellor of the Exchequer,
                    have been on the Continent for the past several weeks. Mr. Dening stated that it
                    is possible that Mr. Bevin, who has not yet seen the memorandum, might <pb
                        facs="0067" n="57" xml:id="pg57"/> repudiate some of the views contained
                    therein, although he doubted it, as he was well acquainted with Mr. Bevin’s
                    views in regard to the Far East.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer>For the Ambassador: <lb/>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Erle</hi> R. <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Dickover</hi>
                    </signed>
                    <lb/>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Counselor of Embassy</hi>
                </closer>


                <xi:include href="d72-a1.xml" xpointer="d72-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
